conv_id=12399 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
TabAtkins: Your use of &lt;b&gt; in css3-images is incorrect per HTML5. Technical terms are supposed to be marked up as &lt;i&gt;.
is reviewing the draft
or rather, parts ofit
Also, don..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
TabAtkins: Your use of &lt;b&gt; in css3-images is incorrect per HTML5. Technical terms are supposed to be marked up as &lt;i&gt;.
is reviewing the draft
or rather, parts ofit
Also, don't reference editor's drafts unless you can't reference the official WD for some reason
you think that is really better?
which?
referencing official WDs?
yeah
depends on how outdated the WD is :)
well yeah, but any WD is outdated by definition :)
didn't say if, but how much
;)
fair enough, but is there a particular reason you think referencing a WD is better?
if the problem is that WDs are not a good reference
then we should get rid of them
and make the editor's draft official
or something
in some cases that's a good idea
in others, not so much
you seem to hinge on this &quot;official&quot; thing quite a bit :)
that's what we're directing people at
as an organization
I'd rather get more rapid updates to WDs
than ad-hoc links, half to WDs and half to EDs
some of which are reasonable references
and others of which are crazy bits of scratch space
yeah, but if you cannot have rapid updates to WDs we could be more practical
we could, if it made sense
organizations and their policies change
but usually not top-down
as you seem to suggest
if what Tab was linking to was something for which the ED was obviously a beter reference
but it's not
it's a reference to a term defined in css3-background
which at this point is quite stable, is not very much out-of-sync from its official copy (other than status)
and it's a term definition in what's practically an example
all the more reason not to worry much about it I would say
I'll have to fix it eventually anyway
I'd rather fix it now, and avoid similar problems in the future, than have &quot;fix all references&quot; on my to-do list when we go to LC
it seems something to fix when background becomes REC
before that there's no really not much difference
imo
why do we care about REC?
the URL isn't going to change when css3-background goes to REC
ideally by then it's stable
I don't want to have to go back and update random unrelated drafts just because one module advanced in status
if it's stable enough to reference the official URL, I want us referencing that
that's already going to be a problem once you get css4-background
or whatever we call it
and if it's not, I want to pester the relevant working group until they get us a stable enough definition at the offical URL
a) CSS4 background isn't going to create a problem here
b) Referencing the editor's draft doesn't solve that problem anyway, since our editor's drafts are level-specific
true true
pestered the media fragments wg until they got us a WD; so this method works :)
sure, but WDs are generally bad for implementors
at least for us
Not going to argue with that.
Ideally the latest version URL would be the editor's draft
or at least something much closer to it
Part of the difficulty is that the CSSWG wants a chance to review the editor's work, at least the major changes, before the draft goes out as official
I don't quite get that
whereas minor changes / bugfixes should just go straight up
but it does indeed seem that a majority of the CSSWG liks reviewing drafts during meetings rather than some other time
So, say I draft a new feature in CSS3 Text
howcome wants a chance to make review that and make comments
and steveZ etc.
before it gets published officially as representing the WG position
yeah I know, but WDs don't represent the WG position and you could just continuously review work you are interested in
they do to some degree
the degree increases as the draft gets more stable
it does seem to help with focus now and then though
e.g. the simplification of the writing modes draft
in the early stages, it's like &quot;we roughly agree that this is an area to work in&quot;
but I'm not sure if that is really thanks to this particular process
then it's &quot;we roughly agree on what these features look like&quot;
then it's &quot;we agree on what these features look like and roughly on their behavior&quot;
then &quot;we agree on these features and their behavior&quot;
and by CR it's locked down to the details
ideally :)
barring unseen bugs, it does represent the WG position down to the details
wrt continuous review
it's very hard to do that
having the editor say &quot;Ok, I'm done reshuffling everything, please take a look, age: i'm ."}"")
