CONVERSATION:
&lt;XVampireX&gt; <a href="http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/">http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/</a> !!!!!!!!!! &lt;- i think that article is misleading... i doubt they cache seed addresses, esp. this info seems wrong: &quot;The new technology is especially helpful in situations where there are only a handful of seed files.&quot; Caches usually operate with MFU queues, thus the most popular content gets cached to lessen the network load w/o caching too many items. So unpopular torrents would profit l
&lt;Nolar&gt; it's not an open spec yet, and it's still unclear as to whether it would provide any benefit to our users atm &lt;- open specs are always better, they allow review and nobody can decide who uses the spec and who doesn't (and it makes it easier for smaller clients to implement it)... of course one could reverse-engineer clients using this protocol, but that's effort which shouldn't be necessary
The_8472:  it's more a question of actual usefulness
i.e. caches dont help unless the isps are on board, and the caches themselves are inside the network
bt inc is using cachelogic as a cdn
(download something from their website with the latest az builds, and you'll see cachelogic peers)
but those caches are *outside* isp networks
that's fine for a for-pay cdn, but unless isps put their boxes inside their networks, it wont be useful for non-pay content
and it's not clear isps will bother
well, we've seen what happend to JPC...
exactly :)
and for ISPs to support it the clients have to support it too, which might happen sooner with an open spec ;)
it's a bit of a catch 22
